"Splinters of Infinity by Mark Wolverton: 9780262048828 | PenguinRandomHouse.com: Books (https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/739509/splinters-of-infinity-by-mark-wolverton/)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\nImage: Splinters of Infinity by Mark Wolverton Image\n\nBest Seller\n\nImage Look Inside\n\n# Splinters of Infinity\n\nImage: Splinters of Infinity by Mark Wolverton\n\nHardcover $29.95\n\nPublished on Mar 12, 2024 | 280 Pages\n\n[Button: Add to Cart] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\nBuy from Other Retailers:\n\nImage: Splinters of Infinity by Mark Wolverton\n\nEbook\n\nPublished on Mar 12, 2024 | 280 Pages\n\nSee All Formats (1) +\n\nHardcover\n\n–\n\n  * [Button: Hardcover ] $29.95\n\nPublished on Mar 12, 2024 | 280 Pages\n\n[Button: Add to Cart] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\n### Book Description Image\n\nThe riveting story of a modern age scientific feud between two Nobel Prize-winning scientists over the nature of cosmic rays and the universe.\nSet in a revolutionary era of physics and science when a series of rapid-fire discoveries was upending our understanding of the universe, Splinters of Infinity by Mark Wolverton tells a little-known story: the tale of two of America’s foremost physicists, Robert Millikan (1868–1953) and Arthur Compton (1892–1962), who found themselves locked in an intense, often deeply personal, conflict about cosmic rays. Confirmed in 1912, cosmic rays—enigmatic forms of penetrating radiation—seemed to raise all new questions about the origins of the universe, but they also offered the potential to explain everything—or reveal the existence of God.\nIn engaging, accessible prose, Wolverton takes the reader through the twists and turns of the Millikan-Compton debate, one of the first major public examples of how heated the controversies among scientists could become—and the lengths that scientists would go to settle their disputes. What set them apart, at least in most cases, Wolverton shows, was their ability to concentrate finally on what mattered: the science. Along the way, Wolverton probes the forever elusive question, still unanswered today, about where cosmic rays come from and what they reveal about black holes, distant galaxies, the existence of dark matter and dark energy, and the birth of the universe, concluding that these splinters of infinity may not hold the keys to the secret of creation but do bring us ever closer to it.\n\n\n#### Related Genres\n\nScience & Technology\n\n### Praise Image\n\n“An interesting reflection of the human nature of science.”\n—Popular Science\n\n[Button: See More ]\n\n### Product Details Image\n\nISBN 9780262048828\n\nPublished on Mar 12, 2024\n\nPublished by The MIT Press\n\nPages 280\n\nDimensions 6 x 9\n\nSplinters of Infinity\n\nBy Mark Wolverton--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProducts | Origins of Genius: Darwinian Perspectives on Creativity | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/book/53744/chapter-abstract/422177434)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\nOrigins of Genius: Darwinian Perspectives on Creativity\n\nDean Keith Simonton\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780195128796.001.0001\n\nPublished:\n\n08 July 1999\n\nOnline ISBN:\n\n9780197736357\n\nPrint ISBN:\n\n9780195128796\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n[Button: Disclosure]\n\nAs an Amazon Associate OUP earns from qualifying purchases.\n\n### Contents\n\n[Input]\n\n  1. Expand Front Matter\n\n  2. 1 Geniuand Darwin\n\n  3. 2 Cognition\n\n  4. 3 Variation\n\n  5. 4 Development\n\n  6. Collapse 5 Products\n\n  7. 6 Groups\n\n  8. 7 Darwinian Genius\n\n  9. Expand End Matter\n\n[Input]\n\n  * < Previous chapter\n  * Next chapter >\n\nOrigins of Genius: Darwinian Perspectives on Creativity\n\nDean Keith Simonton\n\n[Button: Contents ]\n\n[Button: Contents ]\n\n[Input: Search in this book] Search in this book\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\n[Button: Disclosure]\n\nAs an Amazon Associate OUP earns from qualifying purchases.\n\nChapter\n\n# 5 Products\n\nGet access\n\nDean Keith Simonton\n\nDean Keith Simonton\n\nFind on\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780195128796.003.0005\n\nPages\n\n145–197\n\n  * Published:\n\n08 July 1999\n\n## Abstract\n\nIf the genius of modern European civilization is largely defined by such creators as Newton, Shakespeare, Michelangelo, and Beethoven, so are these geniuses themselves mostly defined by the creative products on which their respective reputations are founded. Take away the Principia, the Opticks, and the mathematical essays, and Newton would become just a paranoid and emotionally volatile aficionado of alchemy and esoteric biblical exegesis. If it is ever proven that Shakespeare was, not the true author of the plays and poems currently attached to his name, all that would remain would be a meager residual of mundane facts about an obscure Elizabethan actor. If all of Michelangelo’s artistic and architectural achievements were destroyed in some tragic holocaust, there would survive only a minor writer of Italian sonnets. And Beethoven, in the absence of his concert, chamber, church, and theater compositions, would become a once promising piano virtuoso sadly reduced by progressive deafness to an impoverished alcoholic.\n\nKeywords: Michelangelo, architectural, chamber, alcoholic, holocaust\n\nSubject\n\nCognitive Psychology Philosophy of Mind Popular Health\n\nCollection: Oxford Scholarship Online\n\nYou do not currently have access to this chapter.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCambridge University Press (https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/15095/excerpt/9781108415095_excerpt.pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; \nCambridge University Press\n978-1-108-41509-5 — Raising Children\nDavid F. Lancy \nExcerpt\nMore Information\n2\nCulture and Infancy\nJust over a decade ago, I began to work on a “Herculean task”—no\none else had ever attempted to do it—the review, analysis, and\nsynthesis of the literature in anthropology (later, history was\nadded) on childhood. The lifespan covered ranged from the idea\nof having a child (who makes the decision, how is it made, what\nmarriage has to do with it) to the adolescent’s transition to\nadulthood. As a measure of the scope of this task, the reference\nlist from the most recent edition of the book that ensued from this\nwork, The Anthropology of Childhood, runs to over a hundred\npages with nearly 2,000 distinct sources. The synthesis yielded one\noverarching conclusion: babies, or the children they might\nbecome, are thought of in entirely different ways across this wide\nsample of societies.\nThe subtitle of the book, “Cherubs, Chattel, Changelings,”\nexpresses my view that this variability can be captured in three\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Anthropology of Childhood - David F. Lancy - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Anthropology_of_Childhood.html?id=U0lkEAAAQBAJ)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n# The Anthropology of Childhood\n\nDavid F. LancyCambridge University Press, Mar 10, 2022 - Social Science - 563 pages How are children raised in different cultures? What is the role of children in society? How are families and communities structured around them? Now in its third edition, this deeply engaging book delves into these questions by reviewing and cataloging the findings of over 100 years of anthropological scholarship dealing with childhood and adolescence. It is organized developmentally, moving from infancy through to adolescence and early adulthood, and enriched with anecdotes from ethnography and the daily media, to paint a nuanced and credible picture of childhood in different cultures, past and present. This new edition has been expanded and updated with over 350 new sources, and introduces a number of new topics, including how children learn from the environment, middle childhood, and how culture is 'transmitted' between generations. It remains the essential book to read to understand what it means to be a child in our complex, ever-changing world. [Input]\n\n### Selected pages\n\nPage 70\n\nPage 62\n\nPage 55\n\nPage 61\n\nPage 42\n\n### Contents\n\nWhere Do Children Come from?  | 1\n | \nThe Value of Children  | 27\n | \nTo Make a Child  | 74\n | \nIt Takes a Village  | 115\n | \nMaking Sense  | 161\n | \nOf Marbles and Morals  | 215\n | \nThe Chore Curriculum  | 255\n | \n\nLiving in Limbo  | 308\n | \nTaming the Autonomous Learner  | 339\n | \nElastic Childhood  | 381\n | \nReferences  | 419\n | \nAuthor Index  | 546\n\n### Other editions - View all\n\n | The Anthropology of Childhood: Cherubs, Chattel, Changelings\nDavid F. Lancy\nLimited preview - 2022\n | \n\n | The Anthropology of Childhood: Cherubs, Chattel, Changelings\nDavid F. Lancy\nNo preview available - 2022\n\n### Common terms and phrases\n\nAché activity adolescent adults Africa alloparents American Anthropology apprenticeship baby Bamana become behavior birth boys Cambridge University Press caregivers Chapter Child Development child labor childcare childhood childrearing children learn children's play chimpanzees chore curriculum cognitive Cross-Cultural culture David Developmental early Ethnobiology ethnographic Evolution Evolutionary Evolutionary Psychology example expected father female fertility foraging gender girls Hadza Hewlett household Huaorani Human hunter-gatherer hunting infant infanticide initiation interaction Inuit Journal knowledge Kpelle Kung labor Lancy LeVine living Maasai make-believe males Mandinka marriage Mazahua Michael Michael E modern mother Nancy Scheper-Hughes neontocracy Ngoni nursing observed offspring older Oxford Papua New Guinea parents Perspectives practices pregnancy Psychology reproduction Research rites ritual Rogoff role rural sexual siblings skills social societies strategies street tasks teacher teaching toddlers traditional urban village weaning WEIRD women York young youth\n\n### About the author (2022)\n\nDavid F. Lancy is Emeritus Professor of Anthropology at Utah State University. Recent publications include Child Helpers (2020) and Raising Children (2017).\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | The Anthropology of Childhood\nAuthor  | David F. Lancy\nEdition  | 3, revised\nPublisher  | Cambridge University Press, 2022\nISBN  | 1108943950, 9781108943956\nLength  | 563 pages\nSubjects  | Social Science › Anthropology › Cultural & Social--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPossiplex: Movies, Intellect, Creative Control, My Computer Life And The Fight For Civilization: An Autobiography Of Ted Nelson (https://works.swarthmore.edu/alum-books/3069/)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\nImage: Possiplex: Movies, Intellect, Creative Control, My Computer Life And The Fight For Civilization: An Autobiography Of Ted Nelson\n\n#### Title\n\n### Possiplex: Movies, Intellect, Creative Control, My Computer Life And The Fight For Civilization: An Autobiography Of Ted Nelson\n\n#### Authors\n\nTheodor H. Nelson , 1959\n\n#### Files\n\n#### ISBN\n\n9780893470043\n\n#### Publication Date\n\n2010\n\n#### Publisher\n\nMindful Press\n\n#### City\n\nHackettstown, NJ\n\n#### Keywords\n\nClass of 1959\n\n#### Comments\n\nLearn more about this work.\n\n#### Recommended Citation\n\nNelson, Theodor H. , 1959, \"Possiplex: Movies, Intellect, Creative Control, My Computer Life And The Fight For Civilization: An Autobiography Of Ted Nelson\" (2010). Books by Alumni. 3069.\nhttps://works.swarthmore.edu/alum-books/3069--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOrigins of Genius: Darwinian Perspectives on Creativity - Dean Keith Simonton - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/Origins_of_Genius.html?hl=en&id=LcB2kOXT-68C&output=html_text)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Origins of Genius: Darwinian Perspectives on Creativity\n\nDean Keith SimontonOxford University Press, Jul 8, 1999 - Psychology - 320 pages How can we account for the sudden appearance of such dazzling artists and scientists as Mozart, Shakespeare, Darwin, or Einstein? How can we define such genius? What conditions or personality traits seem to produce exceptionally creative people? Is the association between genius and madness really just a myth? These and many other questions are brilliantly illuminated in The Origins of Genius. Dean Simonton convincingly argues that creativity can best be understood as a Darwinian process of variation and selection. The artist or scientist generates a wealth of ideas, and then subjects these ideas to aesthetic or scientific judgment, selecting only those that have the best chance to survive and reproduce. Indeed, the true test of genius is the ability to bequeath an impressive and influential body of work to future generations. Simonton draws on the latest research into creativity and explores such topics as the personality type of the genius, whether genius is genetic or produced by environment and education, the links between genius and mental illness (Darwin himself was emotionally and mentally unwell), the high incidence of childhood trauma, especially loss of a parent, amongst Nobel Prize winners, the importance of unconscious incubation in creative problem-solving, and much more. Simonton substantiates his theory by examining and quoting from the work of such eminent figures as Henri Poincare, W. H. Auden, Albert Einstein, Marie Curie, Charles Darwin, Niels Bohr, and many others. For anyone intrigued by the spectacular feats of the human mind, The Origins of Genius offers a revolutionary new way of understanding the very nature of creativity.Preview this book »\n\n### From inside the book\n\n[Input][Input][Input][Input] [Input]\n\n### Selected pages\n\nTitle Page\n\nTable of Contents\n\n### Contents\n\nThe Surprising Connections  | 1\n | \nHow Does the Brain Create?  | 25\n | \nIs Genius Brilliantor Mad?  | 75\n | \nAre Geniuses Bornor Made?  | 109\n | \nBy What Works Shall We Know Them?  | 145\n | \nThe Right Place and the Right Time?  | 199\n | \n\nThe Future of an Idea  | 243\n | \nNotes  | 249\n | \nReferences  | 261\n | \nIndex  | 297\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOrigins of Genius - ISBN 9780190284534 | CampusBooks (https://www.campusbooks.com/books/9780190284534-origins-of-genius-darwinian-perspectives-on-creativity)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 27.3 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; \n\n# Origins of Genius\n\nImage: Origins of Genius\n[Button: Add to Bag ]\n\nAuthors: Dean Keith Simonton\nISBN 10: 0190284536\nISBN 13: 9780190284534\nEdition: 1\nReleased: Jul 8th, 1999\nFormat: Kindle Edition (320 pages)\nRelated ISBN: 9780195128796\nRelated ISBN: 9780195351705\n\nOrigins of Genius\nISBN 13: 9780190284534\nISBN: 0190284536\nAuthors: Dean Keith Simonton\nEdition: 1\nPublisher: Oxford University Press Academic US\nFormat: Kindle Edition (320 pages)\nReleased: Jul 8th, 1999\nRelated ISBN: 9780195128796\nRelated ISBN: 9780195351705\n\nWe cannot find any offers for this book\n\nGet an email when this book becomes available\n\n[Input][Input]\n\n[Input: Your Email]\n\n[Button: Notify me]\n\n  * Try one of these links to find your book:\n\nSearch by title for Origins of Genius\n\nSearch for this book in a library\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCambridge University Press (https://assets.cambridge.org/97811088/37781/frontmatter/9781108837781_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; \nCambridge University Press  \n978-1-108-83778-1 — The Anthropology of Childhood  \n3rd Edition  \nFrontmatter  \nMore Information\n\nPreface to the First Edition xix\n\nthat are made about what is “normal” or natural in children’s development are, in fact, quite narrowly culture-bound. Indeed, throughout this work, the formula employed in child development texts will be turned on its head. In these texts, research on middle-class Euro-American children defines the standard and “anecdotes” from anthropological studies illustrate “deviation from the mean.” In the pages that follow, common aspects of Western childhood are examined through the lens of anthropology. This lens reveals that what we take for granted as customary appears to be rather strange when compared with prevailing practices found elsewhere. The goal is not to offer a competing volume to standard child development texts but, rather, to offer a supplement or corrective.\n\nThe alliterative terms in my subtitle suggest three compass points in this landscape. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCiNii Books - The anthropology of childhood : cherubs, chattel, changelings (https://ci.nii.ac.jp/ncid/BB17889775)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \n\n  * 東京家政大学 図書館図書館\n\n: pbk K61/L21 0328168\n\nOPAC\n\n  * Tohoku Univ. Main Library本館\n\n: pbk 00140341730\n\nOPAC\n\n  * Doshisha University Library (Imadegawa)\n\n: pbk 384.5||L9178 172300439\n\nOPAC\n\n  * 福岡大学 図書館\n\n: pbk 384.5/L21/1 2000000317509\n\nOPAC\n\n  * Ritsumeikan University Main Library\n\n: pbk 21000514274, : hardback 12100121870\n\nOPAC\n\n## Search this Book/Journal\n\n  * Calil\n  * WorldCat\n\n## Note\n\nIncludes bibliographical references (p. 411-515) and indexes\n\n  *   * ## Details\n\n  * NCID\n    BB17889775\n  * ISBN\n    \n    * 9781107420984\n    * 9781107072664\n  * LCCN\n    2014014319\n  * Country Code\n    uk\n  * Title Language Code\n    eng\n  * Text Language Code\n    eng\n  * Place of Publication\n    Cambridge [England]\n  * Pages/Volumes\n    xiv, 533 p.\n  * Size\n    23 cm\n  * Classification\n    \n    * LCC : GN482\n    * DC22 : 305.23\n  * Subject Headings\n    \n    * LCSH : Children -- Cross-cultural studies\n\n## Export\n\n  * Export to RefWorks\n\n  * Export to EndNote\n\n  * Export to Mendeley\n\n  * Show Refer/BibIX\n\n  * Show RIS\n\n  * Show BibTeX\n\n  * Show TSV\n\n  * Show ISBD\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Anthropology of Childhood: Cherubs, Chattel, Changelings - David F. Lancy - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=Go7ulSOEKEUC)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# The Anthropology of Childhood: Cherubs, Chattel, Changelings\n\nImage: Front CoverDavid F. LancyCambridge University Press, Nov 13, 2008 - Psychology - 466 pages The raising of children, their role in society, and the degree to which family and community is structured around them, varies quite significantly around the world. The Anthropology of Childhood provides the first comprehensive review of the literature on children from a distinctly anthropological perspective. Bringing together key evidence from cultural anthropology, history, and primate studies, it argues that our common understandings about children are narrowly culture-bound. Whereas dominant society views children as precious, innocent and preternaturally cute 'cherubs', Lancy introduces the reader to societies where children are viewed as unwanted, inconvenient 'changelings', or as desired but pragmatically commoditized 'chattels'. Looking in particular at family structure and reproduction, profiles of children's caretakers, their treatment at different ages, their play, work, schooling, and transition to adulthood, this volume provides a rich, interesting, and original portrait of children in past and contemporary cultures. A 